#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147453 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147390 :question/id #uuid "d1946905-5833-58c2-9a0a-4dfd04eb922b" :question/math-academy-id "e-8963" :question/problem "The substitution $z=y^m$ for some real number $m$ transforms the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + x^3y= \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form $z'(x) + P(x)z(x) = Q(x).$ What is the function $Q(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + p(x)y= q(x)y^n.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x) = x^3, \\qquad q(x) = 1, \\qquad n=-\\dfrac12.\n$$\n\nFirst, we multiply the differential equation by $y^{1/2},$ and we get\n\n$$\ny^{1/2} \\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + x^3y^{3/2}= 1.\\qquad\\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz= y^{1-n} =  y^{3/2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}  = \\dfrac{3}{2}y^{1/2} \\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\ny^{1/2}\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{2}{3}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(\\ast)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + x^3\\,z = 1\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the above equation in standard form, which gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac{3}{2}x^3z = \\dfrac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x) = \\dfrac{3}{2}.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5dc2192f-24a4-5160-844c-4d17096779e5" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048365 :topic/math-academy-id 1164 :topic/title "Reducing ODEs to First-Order Linear by Substitution"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147723 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "186d0c19-d903-54ae-af08-d3ae1a5eb252" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238262} {:answer/id #uuid "4aaf5040-9bb1-5f15-aaf3-d4836f0fc679" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238700} {:answer/id #uuid "98d9b37c-f261-5386-aa13-02f5a695b4c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239382} {:answer/id #uuid "b845640e-7864-59e9-85fd-ffdb6a89bfe8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239660} {:answer/id #uuid "fc0ad406-2f7e-5d20-90a7-561a831321bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240283}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4aaf5040-9bb1-5f15-aaf3-d4836f0fc679" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238700} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7ae302c-36a8-5375-ba5d-947ebfa7e674" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240712}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1480f672-8091-5903-a81e-b755f04694ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-106069" :question/problem "The substitution $z={y}^{m}$ for some integer $m$ transforms the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}y=x{y}^{4}\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)+P(x)z(x)=Q(x).$ What is the function $P(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)={x}^{2},\\,q(x)=x,\\,n=4.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{4},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-4}\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}{y}^{-3}=x.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{-3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-3{y}^{-4}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-4}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+{x}^{2}z=x\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the above equation in standard form, which gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-3{x}^{2}z=-3x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=-3{x}^{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147724 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0152f8f7-2919-5e62-9d08-52bb7a0aa144" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238092} {:answer/id #uuid "127581ed-48f8-548e-8e57-417c6d76c08b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238209} {:answer/id #uuid "5588de14-4a7e-5282-8d89-7c4be377ce20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238794} {:answer/id #uuid "8b18979b-02a8-536d-a7e9-b6ee139da31a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239263} {:answer/id #uuid "d69f1982-2eef-5f61-8533-78b91415f605" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239930}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5588de14-4a7e-5282-8d89-7c4be377ce20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238794} :answer-field/id #uuid "e019cc4e-185b-5dc6-bb51-568de66fd7c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240723}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b36fbf4d-bb56-53ea-bcd3-bd3fbb7b094e" :question/math-academy-id "q-106070" :question/problem "The substitution $z={y}^{m}$ for some real number $m$ transforms the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+\\frac{y}{x}=x\\sqrt{y}\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)+P(x)z(x)=Q(x).$ What is the function $Q(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,q(x)=x,\\,n=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{1/2},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-1/2}\\frac{dy}{dx}+\\frac{{y}^{1/2}}{x}=x.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{1/2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{-1/2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-1/2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}\\,z=x\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the above equation in standard form, which gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{2x}\\,z=\\frac{x}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x)=\\frac{x}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241152 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "349f818c-cf20-52dc-9e97-0c025ac3a392" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238509} {:answer/id #uuid "50ea1908-2fd7-5a72-a62d-e83e75a0136e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{x}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238754} {:answer/id #uuid "7d93a487-2430-58d6-9642-e38fc1ebdfe9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6{x}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239144} {:answer/id #uuid "884fd422-3560-570a-b65b-552b55c60c84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6{x}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239240} {:answer/id #uuid "f14373e5-d988-5875-8ac6-9582fc8d6e6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240185}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "884fd422-3560-570a-b65b-552b55c60c84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6{x}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239240} :answer-field/id #uuid "9689e5da-04b7-54b3-886d-0a298bc8dbfb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240595}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "edb9caeb-1ac6-5c8a-b8d9-e751c89dc231" :question/math-academy-id "q-106071" :question/problem "The substitution $z={y}^{m}$ for some integer $m$ transforms the differential equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{dy}{dx}-y(2x+3{y}^{2})=0\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)+P(x)z(x)=Q(x).$ What is the function $Q(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{dy}{dx}-y(2x+3{y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}\\,\\frac{dy}{dx}-2xy-3{y}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}\\,\\frac{dy}{dx}-2xy & =3{y}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx}-2{x}^{-1}y & =3{x}^{-2}{y}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-2{x}^{-1},\\,q(x)=3{x}^{-2},\\,n=3.\n$$\n\nNow, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{3},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-3}\\frac{dy}{dx}-2{x}^{-1}{y}^{-2}=3{x}^{-2}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{-2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-2{y}^{-3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-2{x}^{-1}z=3{x}^{-2}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the above equation in standard form, which gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+4{x}^{-1}z=-6{x}^{-2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x)=-6{x}^{-2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241153 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20139243-a9a4-560d-acc5-15c4919cf194" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238332} {:answer/id #uuid "3e6c3a78-79fe-509f-a0a3-7fd88f29faff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238600} {:answer/id #uuid "52cc1be9-4991-5a91-91fa-7a3527a91a46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238768} {:answer/id #uuid "7057f0ac-cd18-513a-b5d8-f94b0dee9790" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239024} {:answer/id #uuid "b473f221-aaac-56e6-bcf2-eb9dc5fa9c90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239633}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b473f221-aaac-56e6-bcf2-eb9dc5fa9c90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239633} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d5972f4-febe-5279-a64b-8a127170a62b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240414}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7793e790-8241-5316-bdc4-fcf549de3fa7" :question/math-academy-id "q-106072" :question/problem "The substitution $z={y}^{m}$ for some real number $m$ transform the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{1/2}\\,\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}{y}^{3/2}-y=0\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)+P(x)z(x)=Q(x).$ What is the function $P(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{1/2}\\,\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}{y}^{3/2}-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{1/2}\\,\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}{y}^{3/2} & =y \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}y & ={y}^{1/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)={x}^{2},\\,q(x)=1,\\,n=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{1/2},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-1/2}\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}{y}^{1/2}=1.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{1/2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{-1/2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-1/2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+{x}^{2}z=1\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the above equation in standard form, which gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\,z=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244210 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a3b6a83-51f2-5e59-b21f-fb63d5e6a316" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241994} {:answer/id #uuid "620d1dfc-933b-57d8-a4c7-a454bf226579" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242060} {:answer/id #uuid "78e5e9c2-5760-50f0-860f-a1bb2ae2e017" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242246} {:answer/id #uuid "8a46ee75-f31f-564a-a048-e472d45e83d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242397} {:answer/id #uuid "cc80adbb-6dc1-5082-a820-d0687f2295e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242976}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78e5e9c2-5760-50f0-860f-a1bb2ae2e017" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242246} :answer-field/id #uuid "c659286f-433b-59c7-97df-c22a324022a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243789}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ac82a1e5-f377-51b8-8786-06db64bfa2d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-109009" :question/problem "The substitution $z={y}^{m}$ for some integer $m$ transforms the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}-y\\operatorname{tan}⁡x={y}^{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)+P(x)z(x)=Q(x).$ What is the function $Q(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,\\,q(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,n=5.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{5},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-5}\\frac{dy}{dx}-{y}^{-4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{-4}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-4{y}^{-5}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-5}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{4}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-z\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the above equation in standard form, which gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+4z\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x)=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244211 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a55f401-8a5d-5ece-a5fe-615210e9edc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241271} {:answer/id #uuid "1444c17a-6021-5368-afcd-d8e00c978f3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241352} {:answer/id #uuid "38bb4d1c-4f04-5cd4-86a7-64f140b6e862" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241688} {:answer/id #uuid "51346454-15ef-58bd-a1f6-c9f6cfb74650" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241906} {:answer/id #uuid "9c9f50f2-6be3-5535-890c-4ee848e37967" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242548}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1444c17a-6021-5368-afcd-d8e00c978f3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241352} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8e5fcc7-2925-5436-a36f-d02653fb6d2e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243843}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "59ec66ad-ab07-5df0-ac9e-4d8b85fb44fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-109014" :question/problem "The substitution $z={y}^{m}$ for some integer $m$ transforms the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+3xy=-{x}^{4}{y}^{3}\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)+P(x)z(x)=Q(x).$ What is the function $P(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=3x,\\,q(x)=-{x}^{4},\\,n=3.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{3},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-3}\\frac{dy}{dx}+3x{y}^{-2}=-{x}^{4}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{-2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-2{y}^{-3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+3xz=-{x}^{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the above equation in standard form, which gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-6xz=2{x}^{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=-6x.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244212 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "169b564d-3e13-5ea4-bb02-e06b9cd09064" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241374} {:answer/id #uuid "2d1cffbd-d738-5188-a74c-fef25b9b9e7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241582} {:answer/id #uuid "4de07c15-a05d-551f-9555-4d9adee8d9c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241878} {:answer/id #uuid "69a9b0ff-cd60-5868-b184-1abb3ec51733" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242128} {:answer/id #uuid "e2f347c2-b0c9-5bfd-afd5-d4e38b2e0825" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243195}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2f347c2-b0c9-5bfd-afd5-d4e38b2e0825" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243195} :answer-field/id #uuid "90d1cef5-66ec-5173-b5f0-674d6c0459b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243709}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1fd9f6f1-19c3-5312-8ab5-3e759ad7a9dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-109018" :question/problem "The substitution $z={y}^{m}$ for some real number $m$ transforms the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{4}y=\\sqrt{y}\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)+P(x)z(x)=Q(x).$ What is the function $P(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)={x}^{4},\\,q(x)=1,\\,n=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{1/2},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-1/2}\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{4}{y}^{1/2}=1.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{1/2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{-1/2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-1/2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+{x}^{4}z=1\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the above equation in standard form, which gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}z=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244214 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96b063f9-bfed-5c51-813f-af89adcac919" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242501} {:answer/id #uuid "c2c7c329-7d38-5038-9d1b-3c6399cdcbfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242892} {:answer/id #uuid "f1b146d9-3c67-5e61-a2f8-227f539a7e7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243323} {:answer/id #uuid "f619bae3-2931-5da1-abb8-e9b62912135a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243358} {:answer/id #uuid "fc206c53-9f08-5191-9f03-de93baac3c5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243401}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc206c53-9f08-5191-9f03-de93baac3c5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243401} :answer-field/id #uuid "ecfdd8ef-02ec-5d37-99d2-f1f761e9d08b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243847}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "97e8bc76-2e14-55a4-986a-6a5e78385db7" :question/math-academy-id "q-109021" :question/problem "The substitution $z={y}^{m}$ for some real number $m$ transforms the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+\\frac{y}{x}={x}^{3}{y}^{3/4}\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)+P(x)z(x)=Q(x).$ What is the function $Q(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,q(x)={x}^{3},\\,n=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{3/4},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-3/4}\\frac{dy}{dx}+\\frac{{y}^{1/4}}{x}={x}^{3}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{1/4}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{-3/4}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-3/4}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n4\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}\\,z={x}^{3}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the above equation in standard form, which gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{4x}\\,z=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x)=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244215 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24bb227d-4dda-5d52-9d71-451714452682" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241498} {:answer/id #uuid "5c87aa48-c039-5e40-9bdd-4347ae47cebd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242015} {:answer/id #uuid "9117c120-c690-5098-bbaa-c131be9a1c19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242453} {:answer/id #uuid "b97245ce-f731-5b15-9471-b77ef86aa332" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242808} {:answer/id #uuid "f2a95fdc-c9ab-523b-aafd-b92557b122c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243331}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24bb227d-4dda-5d52-9d71-451714452682" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241498} :answer-field/id #uuid "90a69aac-1d1d-5e00-9801-9850ef8c433c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243708}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "59b54a0e-f75c-5a06-aa23-4e21a8ecaf83" :question/math-academy-id "q-109022" :question/problem "The substitution $z={y}^{m}$ for some real number $m$ transforms the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}y=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{\\sqrt{y}}\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)+P(x)z(x)=Q(x).$ What is the function $P(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)={x}^{2},\\,q(x)={x}^{3},\\,n=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we multiply the differential equation by ${y}^{1/2},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{1/2}\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}{y}^{3/2}={x}^{3}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{3/2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{1/2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{1/2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+{x}^{2}\\,z={x}^{3}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the above equation in standard form, which gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}\\,z=\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371548 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d1dd27a-f2d1-518f-aaf0-11391043ab60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371550} {:answer/id #uuid "35c9a700-5e24-5f4b-b032-0cb7cd2d879c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371551} {:answer/id #uuid "8358d758-0e97-5f01-bb8e-0c3fae7282d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371552} {:answer/id #uuid "39d2841c-04ce-50b3-9951-6e0a2fd427d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371553} {:answer/id #uuid "4dd4eee5-51a0-5f94-ab98-a8758174a582" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371554}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8358d758-0e97-5f01-bb8e-0c3fae7282d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371552} :answer-field/id #uuid "a50b330c-f87d-5c0a-8d36-ef8cd39ea565" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371549}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "972cc08b-2d79-5a95-8b03-a6f20f3836b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-109003" :question/problem "The substitution $z={y}^{m}$ for some integer $m$ transforms the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+y=-\\frac{x}{y}\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)+P(x)z(x)=Q(x).$ What is the function $Q(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=1,\\,q(x)=-x,\\,n=-1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we multiply the differential equation by $y,$ and we get\n\n$$\ny\\frac{dy}{dx}+{y}^{2}=-x.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=2y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z=-x\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the above equation in standard form, which gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+2z=-2x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x)=-2x.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386343 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f87ce40-e494-57fd-87db-da5021c176f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3{x}^{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386345} {:answer/id #uuid "6b18064b-d5d5-5078-a8fd-b6418e621786" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386346} {:answer/id #uuid "92694b8d-752a-5dcd-916a-6780faed7a53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2x}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386347} {:answer/id #uuid "6a22edc0-b75f-5ea1-b0f5-29d17967c9fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386348} {:answer/id #uuid "583c9117-272c-5a9e-be49-c56f6acded69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386349}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92694b8d-752a-5dcd-916a-6780faed7a53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2x}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386347} :answer-field/id #uuid "4750c585-5e91-52f3-8d29-0793fe4fcf35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386344}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "023ae6da-320c-5d9f-893c-3cafd754c018" :question/math-academy-id "q-109024" :question/problem "The substitution $z={y}^{m}$ for some real number $m$ transforms the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}-xy=\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{y}}{{x}^{5}}\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)+P(x)z(x)=Q(x).$ What is the function $P(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-x,\\,q(x)={x}^{-5},\\,n=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{1/3},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-1/3}\\frac{dy}{dx}-x{y}^{2/3}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{2/3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{-1/3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-1/3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-x\\,z=\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the above equation in standard form, which gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{2x}{3}\\,z=\\frac{2}{3{x}^{5}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=-\\frac{2x}{3}.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Bernoulli Differential Equation to First Order Linear"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147454 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147391 :question/id #uuid "047ea3c1-8ebf-5e26-9f28-c5a87e7cf997" :question/math-academy-id "e-8965" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac{y}{x}  = \\dfrac{x^2}{y} .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + p(x)y= q(x)y^n.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}, \\qquad q(x) = x^2, \\qquad n=-1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by $y^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\ny \\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + \\dfrac{y^{2}}{x}= x^2.\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz= y^{1-n} =  y^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}  = 2y \\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\ny\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac{1}{x}z  = x^2\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac{2}{x} z\n\t\t=2x^2\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)z = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{2}{x},\\,Q(x)=2{x}^{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (2/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)=x^2$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{2}{x}z) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot (2{x}^{2}) \\\\ {x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+2xz & =2{x}^{4} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{x}^{2}) & =2{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ z{x}^{2} & =\\frac{2{x}^{5}}{5}+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =\\frac{2{x}^{5}+C}{5{x}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=5C_1.$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z=y^{2}.$ This results in the general solution\n\n$$\ny^{ 2} =\\dfrac{2x^5 + C}{5x^2}.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "17061f4e-da7c-58f9-90b4-47bdc21505da" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147725 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4fb6e0b5-50bd-5bc3-9926-8cce1ea815dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2{e}^{x}}{C-{e}^{2x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238740} {:answer/id #uuid "5b195b37-4d20-505c-ae89-b9a443329f6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{C-{e}^{x}}{2{e}^{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238845} {:answer/id #uuid "8ee3123a-b991-57e4-b166-c66adaa44398" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{C-{e}^{2x}}{2{e}^{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239299} {:answer/id #uuid "d0593637-ef94-5572-897c-c8ab00cf7d80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{C-2{e}^{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239879} {:answer/id #uuid "f02c7ad2-ea02-5ae9-b738-dc45f744f891" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2x{e}^{x}}{C-2{e}^{2x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240170}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4fb6e0b5-50bd-5bc3-9926-8cce1ea815dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2{e}^{x}}{C-{e}^{2x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238740} :answer-field/id #uuid "192366d6-0728-54fb-85fb-3ed3b5b60e0a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240368}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ad742d4e-8bb3-5b2d-a572-286f9bbe2b5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-106073" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-y={y}^{2}{e}^{x}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-1,\\,q(x)={e}^{x},\\,n=2.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{2},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-2}\\frac{dy}{dx}-{y}^{-1}={e}^{x}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-{y}^{-2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-z={e}^{x}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z=-{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=1,\\,Q(x)=-{e}^{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (1)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={e}^{x}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z) & ={e}^{x}\\cdot (-{e}^{x}) \\\\ {e}^{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z{e}^{x} & =-{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{x}) & =-{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ z{e}^{x} & =-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =\\frac{C-{e}^{2x}}{2{e}^{x}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=2{C}_{1}.$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-1}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{-1}=\\frac{C-{e}^{2x}}{2{e}^{x}}\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{2{e}^{x}}{C-{e}^{2x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147726 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd114fc0-127b-5ead-b00d-fe9bffb132d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = (\\frac{2 + x^{3}}{C + x^{2}})^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144120} {:answer/id #uuid "f165fd86-7b5d-5972-b661-153f3789a251" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{C - 2x^{2}}{3x^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144121} {:answer/id #uuid "0435f5b5-1868-5ec4-ab51-b968a42aecfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{C + 3x^{2}}{x^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144122} {:answer/id #uuid "1756d73e-167c-5559-8f61-0cad7f13c45d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{2x^{3}}{C - 2x^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144123} {:answer/id #uuid "5d8e9606-7e84-5b69-bc8a-f9aeef6bf9b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{x^{3}}{C - x^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144124}] :answer-field/id #uuid "e47c4c86-e1e5-5928-8e04-a5acbcf9617c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144119}] :question/id #uuid "caf692da-64e7-571b-8ecf-524b4723203b" :question/math-academy-id "q-106074" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n$x^{2}\\frac{dy}{dx} = y^{4} - xy$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244216 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1db95ec7-7bbb-59e1-8146-28cacbdd7712" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{1+C{e}^{{x}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241433} {:answer/id #uuid "22504129-485e-5c7b-bb3a-4e7932845def" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{1+C{e}^{{x}^{2}/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241478} {:answer/id #uuid "61185df4-4db8-5659-8962-34e04a46e0d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{1+C{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242056} {:answer/id #uuid "62160cf8-9638-5701-94fd-5b00ed372c4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{1+C{e}^{-{x}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242061} {:answer/id #uuid "d43a211a-1155-59ad-8d25-261eacabadfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{e}^{{x}^{2}/2}}{C+x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243056}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22504129-485e-5c7b-bb3a-4e7932845def" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{1+C{e}^{{x}^{2}/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241478} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b4d8005-4bbc-5ae9-99cf-152f6946a657" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243726}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "589d9965-97b7-5fc2-a133-6802310284c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-109026" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+xy=x{y}^{2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=x,\\,q(x)=x,\\,n=2.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{2},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-2}\\frac{dy}{dx}+x{y}^{-1}=x.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-{y}^{-2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+xz=x\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-xz=-x\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=-x,\\,Q(x)=-x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (-x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-xz) & ={e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}\\cdot (-x) \\\\ {e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-xz{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2} & =-x{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}) & =-x{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2})\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int x{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ z{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2} & ={e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}+C \\\\ z & =1+C{e}^{{x}^{2}/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-1}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{-1}=1+C{e}^{{x}^{2}/2}\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{1}{1+C{e}^{{x}^{2}/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244217 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "082efab0-0317-5dbc-abdd-5b8e3e2944a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{C{e}^{3{x}^{2}}-1}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241248} {:answer/id #uuid "1d96bd59-970e-5488-afd5-3d367f724a52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{C-{e}^{3{x}^{2}}}{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241432} {:answer/id #uuid "5d5028bc-2a5a-5f66-ab80-bab0735a9a64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\sqrt[5]{\\frac{C{e}^{5{x}^{3}}-1}{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242021} {:answer/id #uuid "7a832465-fff4-50d1-8849-9ef36b8e1628" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\sqrt[5]{\\frac{C{e}^{x}-1}{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242256} {:answer/id #uuid "9559d545-a9cd-50cf-b2d8-3770de68727e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\sqrt[5]{\\frac{C-{e}^{5{x}^{3}}}{{x}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242489}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d5028bc-2a5a-5f66-ab80-bab0735a9a64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\sqrt[5]{\\frac{C{e}^{5{x}^{3}}-1}{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242021} :answer-field/id #uuid "98b4dff1-92e9-52c3-8bdc-adcfa5fe0d5b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243721}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dc20c4d3-6f46-5d1b-920a-dfd533b0b1fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-109027" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3{x}^{2}y={x}^{2}{y}^{-4}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-3{x}^{2},\\,q(x)={x}^{2},\\,n=-4.\n$$\n\nFirst, we multiply the differential equation by ${y}^{4},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{4}\\frac{dy}{dx}-3{x}^{2}{y}^{5}={x}^{2}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{5}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=5{y}^{4}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-3{x}^{2}z={x}^{2}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-15{x}^{2}z=5{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=-15{x}^{2},\\,Q(x)=5{x}^{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (-15{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-5{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={e}^{-5{x}^{3}}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-5{x}^{3}}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-15{x}^{2}z) & ={e}^{-5{x}^{3}}\\cdot (5{x}^{2}) \\\\ {e}^{-5{x}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-15{x}^{2}z{e}^{-5{x}^{3}} & =5{x}^{2}{e}^{-5{x}^{3}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{-5{x}^{3}}) & =5{x}^{2}{e}^{-5{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{-5{x}^{3}})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 5{x}^{2}{e}^{-5{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ z{e}^{-5{x}^{3}} & =-\\frac{{e}^{-5{x}^{3}}}{3}+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =\\frac{C{e}^{5{x}^{3}}-1}{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=3{C}_{1}.$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{5}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{5}=\\frac{C{e}^{5{x}^{3}}-1}{3}\\,⇒\\,y=\\sqrt[5]{\\frac{C{e}^{5{x}^{3}}-1}{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244218 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cb78f3a-989b-501c-bd49-e05a5a9fbb84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{C{e}^{x}-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241289} {:answer/id #uuid "797506fa-84e9-5eb1-9fea-8e1b2a741cd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{C{e}^{2x}-2x-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242250} {:answer/id #uuid "bbd2281f-a36b-54cd-bf13-2a97fd60cb3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{2x}{C+{e}^{2x}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242825} {:answer/id #uuid "d3b5b9a5-ce13-5522-b61f-93f41e10347c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2x+1}{C-{e}^{2x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243050} {:answer/id #uuid "d42539a6-6f41-51b8-a98e-7a3a4c4fb5c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{C{e}^{x}-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243054}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "797506fa-84e9-5eb1-9fea-8e1b2a741cd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{C{e}^{2x}-2x-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242250} :answer-field/id #uuid "01d98ddd-fb82-5dc3-b99b-bbb17784af63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243437}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "58944fcc-b27a-50b8-9784-20e0e51f15da" :question/math-academy-id "q-109028" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=-x{y}^{3}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=1,\\,q(x)=-x,\\,n=3.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{3},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-3}\\frac{dy}{dx}+{y}^{-2}=-x.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{-2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-2{y}^{-3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z=-x\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-2z=2x\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=-2,\\,Q(x)=2x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (-2)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={e}^{-2x}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2x}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-2z) & ={e}^{-2x}\\cdot (2x) \\\\ {e}^{-2x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-2z{e}^{-2x} & =2x{e}^{-2x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{-2x}) & =2x{e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{-2x})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2x{e}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ z{e}^{-2x} & =-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-2x}(2x+1)+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =\\frac{C{e}^{2x}-2x-1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=2{C}_{1}.$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-2}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{-2}=\\frac{C{e}^{2x}-2x-1}{2}\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{C{e}^{2x}-2x-1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244219 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2db67a4b-551b-50d3-aa9d-94998480bd57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{x}{C{e}^{x/2}-1})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241586} {:answer/id #uuid "46aaacb9-f86c-5ffc-ac24-3c6368262bf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(C{e}^{x/2}-1)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241818} {:answer/id #uuid "51b19b15-add7-5c7e-8009-0bf343fb02a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{C{e}^{2x}+x+1}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241910} {:answer/id #uuid "d004786f-80c4-57b9-b27c-963af74bb669" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{{e}^{2x}+C}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243012} {:answer/id #uuid "fb1e0800-7084-5f24-ae7c-61d79f458bb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={({e}^{x/2}-2x+C)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243393}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46aaacb9-f86c-5ffc-ac24-3c6368262bf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(C{e}^{x/2}-1)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241818} :answer-field/id #uuid "74c6f198-e482-574d-8ea3-6f80192a57d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243667}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f2cab3ea-07f6-5b6d-87cc-2af665f5707a" :question/math-academy-id "q-109029" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-y=\\sqrt{y}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-1,\\,q(x)=1,\\,n=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{1/2},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-1/2}\\frac{dy}{dx}-{y}^{1/2}=1.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{1/2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{-1/2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-1/2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-z=1\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{2}z=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=-\\frac{1}{2},\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (-1/2)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-x/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={e}^{-x/2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x/2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{2}z) & ={e}^{-x/2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ {e}^{-x/2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{2}z{e}^{-x/2} & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-x/2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{-x/2}) & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-x/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{-x/2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ z{e}^{-x/2} & =-{e}^{-x/2}+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =C{e}^{x/2}-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C={C}_{1}.$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{1/2}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{1/2}=C{e}^{x/2}-1\\,⇒\\,y={(C{e}^{x/2}-1)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244240 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05dccc3a-055f-56b3-b883-95c2ad00bc81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{5x}{C+{x}^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241230} {:answer/id #uuid "086185d1-e93f-51d1-8b6d-eaa525ad35a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{C-5{x}^{5/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241252} {:answer/id #uuid "1d85751b-a7a9-5fd6-a5c7-c8956884f0d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{5x}{C-2{x}^{5/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241430} {:answer/id #uuid "92878b28-57d2-57f6-8d59-8df96602fa63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{2x}{C-{x}^{3/2}})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242465} {:answer/id #uuid "e5d5a33a-0925-57f4-811a-6f47f2738dcd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{x}{C+{x}^{5/2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243221}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d85751b-a7a9-5fd6-a5c7-c8956884f0d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{5x}{C-2{x}^{5/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241430} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b754363-aaef-5ee9-923d-a786dd88ca8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243678}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "48fa1d56-7545-55a4-8175-861ef17502b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-109154" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{y}{x}=\\sqrt{x}\\,{y}^{2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-\\frac{1}{x},\\,q(x)=\\sqrt{x},\\,n=2.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{2},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-2}\\frac{dy}{dx}-\\frac{1}{x}{y}^{-1}=\\sqrt{x}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-{y}^{-2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{x}z=\\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}z=-\\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,Q(x)=-\\sqrt{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (1/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)=x$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}z) & =x\\cdot (-\\sqrt{x}) \\\\ x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z & =-x\\sqrt{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz) & =-{x}^{3/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -{x}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ xz & =-\\frac{2{x}^{5/2}}{5}+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =\\frac{C-2{x}^{5/2}}{5x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=5{C}_{1}.$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-1}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{-1}=\\frac{C-2{x}^{5/2}}{5x}\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{5x}{C-2{x}^{5/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244246 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17db3d82-11ec-509d-ab63-0e5193af0c88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{{x}^{5/4}+C}{5{x}^{1/4}})}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241383} {:answer/id #uuid "2c0d3fc8-af2a-5397-a40e-b88eb47a52b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{C{x}^{1/2}-2}{4{x}^{1/2}})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241573} {:answer/id #uuid "601be5e6-b943-5b1d-bccd-fe6ec7de5ec8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{1/4}+C}{4{x}^{9/4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242043} {:answer/id #uuid "7e50f3de-2289-5189-ab71-28ca20cc56e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{{x}^{9/4}+C}{9{x}^{1/4}})}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242289} {:answer/id #uuid "e31a0853-d5e3-5909-a9a1-0d81e266fab8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{4{x}^{5/4}+C}{3{x}^{1/4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243201}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e50f3de-2289-5189-ab71-28ca20cc56e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{{x}^{9/4}+C}{9{x}^{1/4}})}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242289} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f084e80-d302-55b4-bd36-6495164f8e07" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243525}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ad4ebdc1-705f-579a-b327-9d9638977692" :question/math-academy-id "q-109170" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\nx\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y={x}^{2}{y}^{3/4}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y={x}^{2}{y}^{3/4} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{y}{x} & =x{y}^{3/4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)={x}^{-1},\\,q(x)=x,\\,n=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{3/4},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-3/4}\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{-1}{y}^{1/4}=x.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{1/4}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{-3/4}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-3/4}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n4\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+{x}^{-1}z=x\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-1}z=\\frac{x}{4}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is now in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x),\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{1}{4x},\\,Q(x)=\\frac{x}{4}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (1/4x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{(1/4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={x}^{1/4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={x}^{1/4}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{1/4}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-1}z) & ={x}^{1/4}\\cdot (\\frac{x}{4}) \\\\ {x}^{1/4}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-3/4}z & =\\frac{{x}^{5/4}}{4} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{x}^{1/4}) & =\\frac{{x}^{5/4}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{x}^{1/4})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{{x}^{5/4}}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ z{x}^{1/4} & =\\frac{1}{9}{x}^{9/4}+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =\\frac{{x}^{9/4}+C}{9{x}^{1/4}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=9{C}_{1}.$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{1/4}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n{y}^{1/4}=\\frac{{x}^{9/4}+C}{9{x}^{1/4}}\\,⇒\\,y={(\\frac{{x}^{9/4}+C}{9{x}^{1/4}})}^{4}\\,.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244247 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f72fa48-00bb-5f1a-977f-8c3a1854fc12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{C-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241603} {:answer/id #uuid "35f33d3b-7fb6-5684-af24-1fc172ab09e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x+C}{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241650} {:answer/id #uuid "55bcc63a-a6e9-5e69-ac5e-35e6a3036181" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{C-x}{2{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241952} {:answer/id #uuid "d0694046-7042-5d19-85b1-970f97668ed2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{Cx+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243018} {:answer/id #uuid "f06c513f-cd6c-5fd9-894b-b623992985bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{C-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243317}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f72fa48-00bb-5f1a-977f-8c3a1854fc12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{C-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241603} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f7433a5-cbf9-59b6-a9b7-13dcb3678958" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243625}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6a2e7c3e-5114-54f0-aa4a-d1c840050f5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-109172" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}+2xy}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}+2xy}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2{x}^{-1}y & ={x}^{-2}{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-2{x}^{-1},\\,q(x)={x}^{-2},\\,n=2.\n$$\n\nNow, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{2},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-2}\\frac{dy}{dx}-2{x}^{-1}{y}^{-1}={x}^{-2}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-{y}^{-2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-2{x}^{-1}z={x}^{-2}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+2{x}^{-1}z=-{x}^{-2}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is now in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x),\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{2}{x},\\,Q(x)=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (2/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={x}^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+2{x}^{-1}z) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot (-{x}^{-2}) \\\\ {x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+2xz & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}z) & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}z)\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {x}^{2}z & =-x+C \\\\ z & =\\frac{C-x}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-1}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n{y}^{-1}=\\frac{C-x}{{x}^{2}}\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{C-x}\\,.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244248 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "177d975d-e56e-5b9c-9d15-f202564d2dcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{x}^{2/3}-Cx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241381} {:answer/id #uuid "2494ed9c-553b-5032-82dd-4cad1100915e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{(3\\sqrt{x}+C)}^{2/3}}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241497} {:answer/id #uuid "2a0262c3-0c88-53c2-8f6c-a696ff2d9c86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{(3+Cx)}^{2/3}}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241549} {:answer/id #uuid "3ed47f22-7be9-5d13-aafa-9aa435553945" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3\\sqrt{x}+C{x}^{2/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241740} {:answer/id #uuid "b691ba50-56e8-5e37-96ad-51102a634da5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}+C}{x})}^{2/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242778}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2494ed9c-553b-5032-82dd-4cad1100915e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{(3\\sqrt{x}+C)}^{2/3}}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241497} :answer-field/id #uuid "c21b45e9-28a2-59e4-bff7-31ca0f9bf671" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243782}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7c7c063b-3c69-5ffc-9eea-18aa3a923ee9" :question/math-academy-id "q-109177" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}-xy.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={y}^{-1/2}-xy \\\\ {x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+xy & ={y}^{-1/2} \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{-1}y & ={x}^{-2}{y}^{-1/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)={x}^{-1},\\,q(x)={x}^{-2},\\,n=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the differential equation by ${y}^{1/2},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{1/2}\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{-1}{y}^{3/2}={x}^{-2}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{3/2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{1/2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{1/2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+{x}^{-1}z={x}^{-2}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-1}z=\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-2}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is now in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x),\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{3}{2x},\\,Q(x)=\\frac{3}{2{x}^{2}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (3/2x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{(3/2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={x}^{3/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={x}^{3/2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3/2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-1}z) & ={x}^{3/2}\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-2}) \\\\ {x}^{3/2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{1/2}z & =\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3/2}z) & =\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3/2}z)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {x}^{3/2}z & =3{x}^{1/2}+C \\\\ z & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{x}+C}{{x}^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{3/2}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n{y}^{3/2}=\\frac{3\\sqrt{x}+C}{{x}^{3/2}}\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{{(3\\sqrt{x}+C)}^{2/3}}{x}\\,.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399657 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6773b545-4c85-529f-8c68-3b883278b649" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{C-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399659} {:answer/id #uuid "f60a96fe-942a-5e12-a66c-9e48bde3cb0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3x}{C-{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399660} {:answer/id #uuid "fe66776a-5049-554f-b295-b90565404145" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{C+2{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399661} {:answer/id #uuid "e00a7e53-ea54-5b25-9e84-5594638cadcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{3}+C}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399662} {:answer/id #uuid "f849933c-eec0-5349-a930-557222d316e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{C-{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399663}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f60a96fe-942a-5e12-a66c-9e48bde3cb0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3x}{C-{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399660} :answer-field/id #uuid "782ea541-65bb-5fe7-a7fe-a730b04302d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399658}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8cbfb9b8-f301-5858-97fc-1b90fa72d9ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-109158" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{3}{y}^{2}+xy.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={x}^{3}{y}^{2}+xy \\\\ {x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy & ={x}^{3}{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{y}{x} & =x{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-\\frac{1}{x},\\,q(x)=x,\\,n=2.\n$$\n\nNow, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{2},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-2}\\frac{dy}{dx}-\\frac{{y}^{-1}}{x}=x.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-{y}^{-2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{x}z=x\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}z=-x\n$$\n\nThe above equation is now in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x),\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,Q(x)=-x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (1/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)=x$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}z) & =x\\cdot (-x) \\\\ x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z & =-{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz) & =-{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz)\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ xz & =-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =\\frac{C-{x}^{3}}{3x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=3{C}_{1}.$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-1}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n{y}^{-1}=\\frac{C-{x}^{3}}{3x}\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{3x}{C-{x}^{3}}\\,.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Bernoulli Differential Equation"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147456 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147393 :question/id #uuid "b5189ec2-ea1c-5036-9543-b963bd072ab7" :question/math-academy-id "e-9069" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Bernoulli differential equation?\n\n- $y\\,y' + x^2y^2 = e^x y^4$\n\n- $xy' + x^3y = -x^2 y^3$\n\n- $y' + y\\cos{x} = e^{y+1}$" :question/worked-solution "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + p(x)y= q(x)y^n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is a real number.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a Bernoulli differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\ny' + x^2y = e^x y^3\n$$\n\nwhich is a Bernoulli differential equation with\n\n$$\np(x) = x^2, \\qquad q(x) = e^x, \\qquad n=3.\n$$\n\n- Equation II is a Bernoulli differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\ny' + x^2y = -xy^3\n$$\n\nwhich is a Bernoulli differential equation with\n\n$$\np(x) = x^2, \\qquad q(x) =-x, \\qquad n=3.\n$$\n\n- Equation III is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. It is missing the $q(x)y^n$ term.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eef27d2b-6149-5070-b711-f4dcc7db2a03" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049592 :topic/math-academy-id 3215 :topic/title "Introduction to Differential Equations"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147729 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7400fe2-57e2-5a4e-bded-f9b2de6b23cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144138} {:answer/id #uuid "bd2852ec-dbe6-5b4c-ad88-11e744ebde41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144139} {:answer/id #uuid "f0ca9576-4d88-545f-befd-42ba5b33586a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144140} {:answer/id #uuid "f88c153a-a6e9-58ba-bd4f-06467d0c8b99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144141} {:answer/id #uuid "3cdeff55-d770-5e4a-add0-f7f885d99ea9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144142}] :answer-field/id #uuid "3f84493d-f98d-517e-a5a1-fe871019e01f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144137}] :question/id #uuid "7b913b73-c105-5ba7-b06a-20d353f239e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-108446" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Bernoulli differential equation?\n\n1. $y^{′} + x^{2}y = xy^{2}$\n2. $y^{′} + x^{2}y^{2} = xy^{3}$\n3. $y^{′} + x^{2}y = \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{y}}$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147730 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab2f4026-36e6-5fbc-833b-decd18f6a835" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144144} {:answer/id #uuid "cd4faf50-366b-579d-a717-61a2ca691125" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144145} {:answer/id #uuid "015491ea-883d-52af-973e-04ddfc150ce3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the given equations is a Bernoulli differential equation" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144146} {:answer/id #uuid "72a8162a-08dd-5d0b-805f-010927f5c146" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144147} {:answer/id #uuid "771e905b-a148-5e10-8532-569befd71d31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144148}] :answer-field/id #uuid "c43c6790-ab66-5b2b-9d5c-3664185f108c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144143}] :question/id #uuid "5064d2f0-3231-54a8-845d-9b0beda86f87" :question/math-academy-id "q-108447" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Bernoulli differential equation?\n\n1. $yy^{′} + x^{2}y^{3} = e^{x}y^{4}$\n2. $y^{2}y^{′} + x^{2}y^{3} = e^{x}y^{4}$\n3. $y^{′} + x^{2}\\sin y = x\\cos^{3} y$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244191 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb9d536d-0e0e-56d0-b075-65f0dfbd2a4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461497} {:answer/id #uuid "508f9c1d-ff37-59ea-8879-8c3b43a99d9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461498}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "508f9c1d-ff37-59ea-8879-8c3b43a99d9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461498} :answer-field/id #uuid "d05f7a7f-f105-54d3-b92a-8e85aaa18df5" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462502} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10df3356-aa72-55c4-bcd3-8b55d0e35022" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461499} {:answer/id #uuid "ae3cf18f-98e0-5531-8398-e40d7d3f3583" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461500}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae3cf18f-98e0-5531-8398-e40d7d3f3583" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461500} :answer-field/id #uuid "12a30126-3bf0-5ebf-853d-b9c28b6450dc" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462503} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70c6c33b-acc0-53d0-83fb-1320d1026d19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461501} {:answer/id #uuid "55465f4e-feef-55e7-aee1-cf9db4d48e96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461502}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70c6c33b-acc0-53d0-83fb-1320d1026d19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461501} :answer-field/id #uuid "a96caab2-f5ca-53aa-b65f-98a419247edb" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462504}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f464429f-4905-5c37-b6cf-d2ce0cb09dd7" :question/math-academy-id "q-108911" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Bernoulli differential equation?\n- **I.** ${y}^{′}-\\sqrt{x}\\,y=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{y}$\n- **II.** ${y}^{′}+y\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=-\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}$ $$\n- **III.** ${y}^{′}-{y}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x={y}^{6}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is a real number.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a Bernoulli differential equation. In this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-\\sqrt{x},\\,q(x)={x}^{3},\\,n=-1.\n$$\n\n- Equation II is a Bernoulli differential equation. In this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,\\,q(x)=-\\sqrt{x},\\,n=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\n- Equation III is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. It is missing the $p(x)y$ term.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244192 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a2b16ff-480b-5920-a02a-3a1113084ec6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461503} {:answer/id #uuid "bb654b6e-18b2-515f-a240-33d5be06024d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461504}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a2b16ff-480b-5920-a02a-3a1113084ec6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461503} :answer-field/id #uuid "0248c736-0f29-5188-901a-4d4aa3e4ae2c" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462505} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bac7a93d-d937-5618-80f7-910f2907cb9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461505} {:answer/id #uuid "6e24f40f-1611-5210-9da6-6cf12584dfe2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461506}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e24f40f-1611-5210-9da6-6cf12584dfe2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461506} :answer-field/id #uuid "69ef8d6f-1e8a-5807-9ad5-6be9ec18aef6" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462506} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "399fd328-0dff-5b83-ad3e-6eadc53264ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461507} {:answer/id #uuid "f13ba9c6-fd30-5e83-986a-34fb441fe34e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461508}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "399fd328-0dff-5b83-ad3e-6eadc53264ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461507} :answer-field/id #uuid "c969fdd5-7a1f-5912-a7b8-46f92b36dfbd" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462507}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bc73c612-2a32-5b85-8989-8b463754a48c" :question/math-academy-id "q-108913" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Bernoulli differential equation?\n- **I.** ${y}^{′}+{x}^{-2}{y}^{3}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$\n- **II.** ${y}^{′}-{e}^{x}y={x}^{2}{y}^{5}$\n- **III.** ${y}^{′}+{y}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=-x{y}^{3}$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is a real number.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. It is missing both the $p(x)y$ and $q(x){y}^{n}$ terms.\n\n- Equation II is a Bernoulli differential equation. In this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-{e}^{x},\\,q(x)={x}^{2},\\,n=5.\n$$\n\n- Equation III is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. It is missing the $p(x)y$ term.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244193 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4188ff28-f5eb-597b-bef2-216186195b37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461509} {:answer/id #uuid "8c7a7e04-5442-58d1-a701-5ba3498e1a2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461510}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4188ff28-f5eb-597b-bef2-216186195b37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461509} :answer-field/id #uuid "37ec752f-54aa-5e7c-9129-479813fba7dd" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462508} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "161adb6f-f3ba-5dde-82d7-642b483bc333" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461511} {:answer/id #uuid "e5f7c94e-4761-585a-a104-0f0a769debd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461512}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "161adb6f-f3ba-5dde-82d7-642b483bc333" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461511} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2f3c6c8-b230-571d-b71c-668aa2d5950b" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462509} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e046b2a-ea43-5ad1-adc1-d3cb22c366ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461513} {:answer/id #uuid "09667041-9fbf-599c-8de4-f713a34ab018" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461514}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09667041-9fbf-599c-8de4-f713a34ab018" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461514} :answer-field/id #uuid "072f6e88-7786-51e0-b297-e5c7e6409e73" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462510}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e63ce1e7-0f23-5c8c-a5ed-4ae3fc1a265b" :question/math-academy-id "q-108914" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Bernoulli differential equation?\n- **I.** ${y}^{′}+\\sqrt{x}y={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$\n- **II.** ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}=x{y}^{3}$\n- **III.** ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}}$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is a real number.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. It is missing the $q(x){y}^{n}$ terms.\n\n- Equation II is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. It is missing the $p(x)y$ term.\n\n- Equation III is a Bernoulli differential equation. In this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)={x}^{2},\\,q(x)=\\sqrt{x},\\,n=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244194 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75270618-7af7-5381-9066-6d3469656acb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461515} {:answer/id #uuid "67390882-204b-5352-ad1e-ccec65f496a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461516}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75270618-7af7-5381-9066-6d3469656acb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461515} :answer-field/id #uuid "b208e569-82a8-5d09-9cd4-e626e88672d8" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462511} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04b1fb51-971c-5aa9-804d-0ccdfcc7a696" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461517} {:answer/id #uuid "c7880ef3-333f-5868-b08a-8422f8cfd792" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461518}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04b1fb51-971c-5aa9-804d-0ccdfcc7a696" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461517} :answer-field/id #uuid "917fdd71-e52e-5156-b297-f58247d19d0f" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462512} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5587940f-b2d2-50f4-8efe-1abc1acf4aab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461519} {:answer/id #uuid "41170317-0947-57dc-838f-57dc269df7a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461520}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5587940f-b2d2-50f4-8efe-1abc1acf4aab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461519} :answer-field/id #uuid "03ac02f9-35ab-51dd-af88-ea5627438f45" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462513}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c444e682-8c21-501d-82e3-dbe63de4e200" :question/math-academy-id "q-108916" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Bernoulli differential equation?\n- **I.** ${y}^{2}{y}^{′}-x{y}^{5}={y}^{4}$\n- **II.** $y{y}^{′}-x\\sqrt{y}={x}^{2}$\n- **III.** ${y}^{′}+y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is a real number.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. If we attempt to write the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-x{y}^{3}={y}^{2}\n$$\n\nand this is not a Bernoulli differential equation because it is missing the $p(x)y$ term.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. If we attempt to write the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{y}}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}\n$$\n\nand this is not a Bernoulli differential equation because it is missing the $p(x)y$ term.\n\n- Equation III is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. It is missing the $q(x){y}^{n}$ term.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the given equations is a Bernoulli differential equation.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244195 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25a64257-013e-5f8d-b152-d9e7634b93e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461521} {:answer/id #uuid "44f88025-dd1b-5198-a95d-7ba9a5e96e82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461522}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25a64257-013e-5f8d-b152-d9e7634b93e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461521} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4513080-f5de-5d9a-b8bd-7e3281ab4ff6" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462514} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "002f52a6-9b40-5817-806e-04c903f93628" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461523} {:answer/id #uuid "f65c2b84-64a9-52f6-9a3b-93f218800c5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461524}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f65c2b84-64a9-52f6-9a3b-93f218800c5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461524} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4d9626a-f524-53e7-923d-73b8234b228b" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462515} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e652a4bd-a6e3-5008-b2e6-7bbfef7cb7ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461525} {:answer/id #uuid "eda70d64-65fd-54ee-82af-095577f34912" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461526}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e652a4bd-a6e3-5008-b2e6-7bbfef7cb7ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461525} :answer-field/id #uuid "83fc44fd-f73d-55e3-a891-ad000c8c31b2" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462516}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d4b7e54f-2216-5cba-ba81-1d5ec0ebc609" :question/math-academy-id "q-108918" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Bernoulli differential equation?\n- **I.** ${y}^{′}-{e}^{x}{y}^{2}=\\sqrt{y}$\n- **II.** ${y}^{3}{y}^{′}+{y}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=\\sqrt{y}$\n- **III.** $y\\,{y}^{′}+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y={cos}^{2}⁡y$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is a real number.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. It is missing the $p(x)y$ term.\n\n- Equation II is a Bernoulli differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)y={y}^{-5/2}\n$$\n\nwhich is a Bernoulli differential equation with\n\n$$\np(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,\\,q(x)=1,\\,n=-\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\n- Equation III is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation.  If we attempt to write the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+x(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}{y})=\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡y}{y}.\n$$\n\nIt is missing both the $p(x)y$ and $q(x){y}^{n}$ terms.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519845 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25529d51-7d4c-589b-a9a1-4a24a0b50e6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519855} {:answer/id #uuid "b1b44e40-913a-580b-9ee8-f276e953cf51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519894}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25529d51-7d4c-589b-a9a1-4a24a0b50e6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519855} :answer-field/id #uuid "1719e5f7-eb85-5a76-bc95-51bc07547d8b" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519848} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00cb1acf-e472-5fd0-824c-aad2f05210be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519843} {:answer/id #uuid "92d05de7-0a56-530d-91c9-89b642ff40f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519881}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92d05de7-0a56-530d-91c9-89b642ff40f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519881} :answer-field/id #uuid "a40aeef9-b6c2-500f-9cd0-7be6ed86634f" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519886} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "839db738-442f-57e4-a5c6-a94672a10cd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519875} {:answer/id #uuid "a0c16ded-38fd-5b7e-bd9d-12a8cc403dcb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519884}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0c16ded-38fd-5b7e-bd9d-12a8cc403dcb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519884} :answer-field/id #uuid "df6fa41f-6131-5f57-b475-864562ced595" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519902}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "09ed8d3f-e8c4-5182-b1db-e5d98c04df75" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Bernoulli differential equation?\n\nWork on intervals where $x>0$ and $y>0$, and where the displayed coefficients are defined.\n\n- **I.** $y' + 4 \\cdot \\sqrt{x} \\cdot y = 3 \\cdot x^{2} \\cdot \\log{\\left(y \\right)}$\n- **II.** $y' + 2 \\cdot x^{2} \\cdot y^{2} = x \\cdot y^{3}$\n- **III.** $y' + 3 \\cdot x^{2} \\cdot y = \\frac{4 \\cdot \\sqrt{x}}{\\sqrt{y}}$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny'+p(x)y=q(x)y^n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options. Work on the stated positive domain, so dividing by powers of $y$ is valid.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. The normalized equation contains a function of $y$ that is not a single power of $y$.\n\n- Equation II is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. The normalized equation contains two different powers of $y$, neither with exponent 1.\n\n- Equation III is a Bernoulli differential equation. In this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=3 \\cdot x^{2},\\quad q(x)=4 \\cdot \\sqrt{x},\\quad n=- \\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, select III."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519852 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2df1b5ab-ed31-5bd3-8fc8-1dc0ccbc67c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519857} {:answer/id #uuid "76450ef9-648c-5b33-838f-16093077e363" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519874}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2df1b5ab-ed31-5bd3-8fc8-1dc0ccbc67c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519857} :answer-field/id #uuid "0bc965c8-4776-5a91-92f3-2e53b74683f4" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519846} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bcc22c11-ad6e-53a9-ad37-89362071a690" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519895} {:answer/id #uuid "d7fdf271-5129-5df3-a7b8-1e9c85fdbcc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519899}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7fdf271-5129-5df3-a7b8-1e9c85fdbcc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519899} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d419543-53e7-599b-9d42-3d0cf3a99e4a" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519866} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f9b503d-2e05-5443-9427-ec4cfd41a371" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519854} {:answer/id #uuid "c019dbe8-f9d6-53b1-9f3f-9b12e20dc250" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519896}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f9b503d-2e05-5443-9427-ec4cfd41a371" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519854} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc6a8639-f3d8-5d90-9205-383e799912c4" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519910}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1ca4778d-d0ee-5be1-91bb-deb87c2a6870" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Bernoulli differential equation?\n\nWork on intervals where $x>0$ and $y>0$, and where the displayed coefficients are defined.\n\n- **I.** $y' - y^{2} \\cdot e^{x} = 5 \\cdot \\sqrt{y}$\n- **II.** $y^{3}y' + 2 \\cdot y^{4} \\cdot \\log{\\left(x \\right)} = 2 \\cdot \\sqrt{y}$\n- **III.** $yy' + 3 \\cdot x \\cdot \\sin{\\left(y \\right)} = \\cos^{2}{\\left(y \\right)}$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny'+p(x)y=q(x)y^n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options. Work on the stated positive domain, so dividing by powers of $y$ is valid.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. The normalized equation contains two different powers of $y$, neither with exponent 1.\n\n- Equation II is a Bernoulli differential equation. Dividing by $y^{3}$, we get\n\n$$\ny' + 2 \\cdot y \\cdot \\log{\\left(x \\right)} = \\frac{2}{y^{\\frac{5}{2}}}\n$$\n\n In this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=2 \\cdot \\log{\\left(x \\right)},\\quad q(x)=2,\\quad n=- \\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\n- Equation III is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. Dividing by $y$, we get\n\n$$\ny' + \\frac{3 \\cdot x \\cdot \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}}{y} = \\frac{\\cos^{2}{\\left(y \\right)}}{y}\n$$\n\n The normalized equation contains a function of $y$ that is not a single power of $y$.\n\nTherefore, select II."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519860 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f457bc94-632a-5352-992e-e6c5a3424593" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519908} {:answer/id #uuid "f9239b0b-9b82-5bd6-9884-9a3032281b92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519909}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f457bc94-632a-5352-992e-e6c5a3424593" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519908} :answer-field/id #uuid "085ba0ab-a4e5-5515-bf9d-4e1c2f9bf259" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519844} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f2ab4bd-2b5e-5363-b5dc-1ad703f3184a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519863} {:answer/id #uuid "ceaa54f6-1e4d-5baf-bb5e-f802dd30bf8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519898}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ceaa54f6-1e4d-5baf-bb5e-f802dd30bf8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519898} :answer-field/id #uuid "1726f0ce-5eb4-5ac5-b540-d18d2403b11c" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519849} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b3bc395-fe4c-526e-b802-660027798152" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519865} {:answer/id #uuid "ec30e286-0384-5a12-9d88-b913c676a26b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519905}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec30e286-0384-5a12-9d88-b913c676a26b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519905} :answer-field/id #uuid "aecef73e-7b03-515d-8ed8-0ba8a0e9f3b8" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519892}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "41658dd9-b512-54b4-8eaf-1cd8fd36a4fa" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Bernoulli differential equation?\n\nWork on intervals where $x>0$ and $y>0$, and where the displayed coefficients are defined.\n\n- **I.** $y^{2}y' - x \\cdot y^{5} = 4 \\cdot y^{4}$\n- **II.** $yy' - 5 \\cdot x \\cdot \\sqrt{y} = x^{2}$\n- **III.** $y' + 2 \\cdot y \\cdot \\log{\\left(x \\right)} = x \\cdot \\log{\\left(y \\right)}$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny'+p(x)y=q(x)y^n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options. Work on the stated positive domain, so dividing by powers of $y$ is valid.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. Dividing by $y^{2}$, we get\n\n$$\ny' - x \\cdot y^{3} = 4 \\cdot y^{2}\n$$\n\n The normalized equation contains two different powers of $y$, neither with exponent 1.\n\n- Equation II is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. Dividing by $y$, we get\n\n$$\ny' - \\frac{5 \\cdot x}{\\sqrt{y}} = \\frac{x^{2}}{y}\n$$\n\n The normalized equation contains two different powers of $y$, neither with exponent 1.\n\n- Equation III is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. The normalized equation contains a function of $y$ that is not a single power of $y$.\n\nTherefore, select none of the given equations."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519864 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86edd90a-cbf2-5c47-9f02-87079912ec1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519876} {:answer/id #uuid "efb0fac3-488f-5c16-a92b-35827149d2a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519907}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "efb0fac3-488f-5c16-a92b-35827149d2a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519907} :answer-field/id #uuid "74912bc0-52b8-5aa0-b06a-0755387008ec" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519873} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3113515c-1f46-532f-ae88-74c781afb95c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519858} {:answer/id #uuid "c5f3ba73-4c8c-597a-986e-96013abdee10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519897}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3113515c-1f46-532f-ae88-74c781afb95c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519858} :answer-field/id #uuid "90636727-ab0e-5549-b002-2849dcc536b9" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519880} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d1e255e-4ba8-5d4a-872a-060c20e7e9d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519856} {:answer/id #uuid "ff444882-4e8a-5d15-8229-76c1d5a6924b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519912}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff444882-4e8a-5d15-8229-76c1d5a6924b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519912} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0fd8d75-8221-5887-9705-81bb141810ed" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519903}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5442b8d0-4faf-5541-997c-b9a9b40fc3e0" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Bernoulli differential equation?\n\nWork on intervals where $x>0$ and $y>0$, and where the displayed coefficients are defined.\n\n- **I.** $y' + \\sqrt{x} \\cdot y = 2 \\cdot x^{2} \\cdot \\log{\\left(y \\right)}$\n- **II.** $y' + x^{2} \\cdot y^{2} = 4 \\cdot x \\cdot y^{3}$\n- **III.** $y' + 5 \\cdot x^{2} \\cdot y = \\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{\\sqrt{y}}$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny'+p(x)y=q(x)y^n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options. Work on the stated positive domain, so dividing by powers of $y$ is valid.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. The normalized equation contains a function of $y$ that is not a single power of $y$.\n\n- Equation II is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. The normalized equation contains two different powers of $y$, neither with exponent 1.\n\n- Equation III is a Bernoulli differential equation. In this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=5 \\cdot x^{2},\\quad q(x)=\\sqrt{x},\\quad n=- \\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, select III."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519868 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42b0e886-861f-5f24-a07d-d5ccb62ab994" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519861} {:answer/id #uuid "88cb15cd-6ff8-5a88-93cb-37fe932091e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519879}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42b0e886-861f-5f24-a07d-d5ccb62ab994" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519861} :answer-field/id #uuid "af91b94c-0eb9-506e-93d0-903d4893b554" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519893} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3dbf3d9a-2efd-50a9-8b49-746db7572310" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519859} {:answer/id #uuid "a4638151-d753-54a1-a9f3-d192129073b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519887}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3dbf3d9a-2efd-50a9-8b49-746db7572310" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519859} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9e7a0f8-3d9b-5bab-a562-2da45bcfa210" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519900} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b16748f-f5c4-59f1-adc0-055fdb384ee2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519851} {:answer/id #uuid "6cd02773-d136-5dac-b9e3-7d26d2f5517b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519869}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6cd02773-d136-5dac-b9e3-7d26d2f5517b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519869} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7b3d7fc-8e5d-577f-948d-1da2b0382c3c" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519904}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "64fb57dc-e17c-52ab-9105-7fc9ff8e13d4" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Bernoulli differential equation?\n\nWork on intervals where $x>0$ and $y>0$, and where the displayed coefficients are defined.\n\n- **I.** $y' + \\frac{y^{3}}{x^{2}} = 5 \\cdot \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}$\n- **II.** $y' - 2 \\cdot y \\cdot e^{x} = 2 \\cdot x^{2} \\cdot y^{5}$\n- **III.** $y' + 3 \\cdot y^{4} \\cdot \\log{\\left(x \\right)} = - x \\cdot y^{3}$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny'+p(x)y=q(x)y^n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options. Work on the stated positive domain, so dividing by powers of $y$ is valid.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. The normalized equation contains a function of $y$ that is not a single power of $y$.\n\n- Equation II is a Bernoulli differential equation. In this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=- 2 \\cdot e^{x},\\quad q(x)=2 \\cdot x^{2},\\quad n=5.\n$$\n\n- Equation III is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. The normalized equation contains two different powers of $y$, neither with exponent 1.\n\nTherefore, select II."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519891 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9856cd8f-1ecf-5090-b104-4a61b05f6c40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519882} {:answer/id #uuid "fcd67961-35d9-52b9-9614-7f044b5e3dc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519911}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9856cd8f-1ecf-5090-b104-4a61b05f6c40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519882} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d67a8e9-5e92-5864-884f-542d26df09cc" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519853} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "746f231b-c31e-52ec-8372-86806f91864c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519872} {:answer/id #uuid "a833f96f-7658-5c7e-872d-a2f9dcb8e16b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519890}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "746f231b-c31e-52ec-8372-86806f91864c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519872} :answer-field/id #uuid "606f37f2-ccbf-570a-be0c-306b515b0fbb" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519867} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ad9b1b8-d78d-53c9-918c-c359a0595b7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519850} {:answer/id #uuid "8817770a-e70f-55cc-a160-cc3388ebb793" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519877}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ad9b1b8-d78d-53c9-918c-c359a0595b7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519850} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec85d78e-46bb-5696-9523-555403cf0e24" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519906}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ae013de7-6f4e-5b96-9f10-eb4987b6f88b" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Bernoulli differential equation?\n\nWork on intervals where $x>0$ and $y>0$, and where the displayed coefficients are defined.\n\n- **I.** $y^{2}y' - 2 \\cdot x \\cdot y^{5} = 2 \\cdot y^{4}$\n- **II.** $yy' - x \\cdot \\sqrt{y} = 5 \\cdot x^{2}$\n- **III.** $y' + y \\cdot \\log{\\left(x \\right)} = 2 \\cdot x \\cdot \\log{\\left(y \\right)}$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny'+p(x)y=q(x)y^n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options. Work on the stated positive domain, so dividing by powers of $y$ is valid.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. Dividing by $y^{2}$, we get\n\n$$\ny' - 2 \\cdot x \\cdot y^{3} = 2 \\cdot y^{2}\n$$\n\n The normalized equation contains two different powers of $y$, neither with exponent 1.\n\n- Equation II is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. Dividing by $y$, we get\n\n$$\ny' - \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{y}} = \\frac{5 \\cdot x^{2}}{y}\n$$\n\n The normalized equation contains two different powers of $y$, neither with exponent 1.\n\n- Equation III is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. The normalized equation contains a function of $y$ that is not a single power of $y$.\n\nTherefore, select none of the given equations."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519901 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15d69431-018c-5b5e-a000-9cab76c113ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519847} {:answer/id #uuid "9bbb2fdc-ffde-5bb8-b4cc-0a43f19f25e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519883}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15d69431-018c-5b5e-a000-9cab76c113ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519847} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d31c640-54b5-558c-b015-d45299822063" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519862} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a62d92bd-0da5-5ef0-b43b-e763b8d1c247" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519888} {:answer/id #uuid "a64ccefe-8627-5c4e-a65e-dbe768c7fda3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519889}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a64ccefe-8627-5c4e-a65e-dbe768c7fda3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519889} :answer-field/id #uuid "70769b38-d69b-5f8e-91d3-6fa4f6f54d3a" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519870} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73d7c09c-1279-59f9-ac82-7936019068f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519871} {:answer/id #uuid "882e8774-da1d-55db-84f3-841082753ae1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519878}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "882e8774-da1d-55db-84f3-841082753ae1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519878} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3669dae-d26b-5274-956c-3f319637ac07" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519885}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "df2a747d-1fde-548a-9eec-41ea553db412" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Bernoulli differential equation?\n\nWork on intervals where $x>0$ and $y>0$, and where the displayed coefficients are defined.\n\n- **I.** $y' - 2 \\cdot y^{2} \\cdot e^{x} = \\sqrt{y}$\n- **II.** $y^{3}y' + 3 \\cdot y^{4} \\cdot \\log{\\left(x \\right)} = 4 \\cdot \\sqrt{y}$\n- **III.** $yy' + 3 \\cdot x \\cdot \\sin{\\left(y \\right)} = 2 \\cdot \\cos^{2}{\\left(y \\right)}$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny'+p(x)y=q(x)y^n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options. Work on the stated positive domain, so dividing by powers of $y$ is valid.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. The normalized equation contains two different powers of $y$, neither with exponent 1.\n\n- Equation II is a Bernoulli differential equation. Dividing by $y^{3}$, we get\n\n$$\ny' + 3 \\cdot y \\cdot \\log{\\left(x \\right)} = \\frac{4}{y^{\\frac{5}{2}}}\n$$\n\n In this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=3 \\cdot \\log{\\left(x \\right)},\\quad q(x)=4,\\quad n=- \\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\n- Equation III is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. Dividing by $y$, we get\n\n$$\ny' + \\frac{3 \\cdot x \\cdot \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}}{y} = \\frac{2 \\cdot \\cos^{2}{\\left(y \\right)}}{y}\n$$\n\n The normalized equation contains a function of $y$ that is not a single power of $y$.\n\nTherefore, select II."}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Bernoulli Differential Equations"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184171 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184070 :question/id #uuid "10cc01a6-af19-5340-9743-ade4d5e252dc" :question/math-academy-id "e-5425" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $3\\times 5$ matrix. If $\\mathcal{B}= \\{\\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3}\\}$ is a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A)$, find $\\textrm{dim(Null}(A)).$" :question/worked-solution "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A) + \\textrm{nullity}(A) = n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=5,$ and since $\\mathcal{B}$ is formed by $3$ vectors, $\\textrm{rank}(A) = 3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 3+\\text{nullity}(A) & =5 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =5-3 \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d562ea0c-94f3-5fe6-ba82-c651a51d0bbe" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048823 :topic/math-academy-id 1856 :topic/title "Finding a Basis of the Null Space of a Matrix"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049009 :topic/math-academy-id 2623 :topic/title "Finding a Basis of the Column Space of a Matrix"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184759 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c2c253b-eb4a-53cb-b0ca-7c84c3094ed1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183164} {:answer/id #uuid "a1905378-ce91-51d4-9b0f-bc5b3501e65e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183165} {:answer/id #uuid "3829fa6b-44cd-591e-a98f-f251876ca514" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183166} {:answer/id #uuid "44fd0839-e352-55e5-a108-928603beaa61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183167} {:answer/id #uuid "499609e4-1cbc-5b03-a3fc-4d0860e6c9f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183168}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "499609e4-1cbc-5b03-a3fc-4d0860e6c9f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183168} :answer-field/id #uuid "229b5487-34b1-598b-9b5d-3ffee9c57d59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183163}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "67b28820-a085-5f18-884d-33b6d40e4f35" :question/math-academy-id "q-35306" :question/problem "Find $\\text{rank}(A)$ given that $\\text{Null}(A)=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2}+{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\,|\\,\\,{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=4,$ since this is the number of components in each of the vectors of the null space. \n\nAlso, since \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\{{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,|\\,\\,{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R\\}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $\\text{nullity}(A)=2.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+2 & =4 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =4-2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184760 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "abafdde9-cae5-582c-8e9f-4abfcfbcb322" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183170} {:answer/id #uuid "d1d831b4-ce0f-5ab0-a85d-095692b8ec9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183171} {:answer/id #uuid "e3aec90e-a80b-5dc9-8aa0-32459cfe2ca8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183172} {:answer/id #uuid "ed949a79-7417-534c-9f56-fa03f1c6bc81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183173} {:answer/id #uuid "15b05d56-f68c-51c4-80b9-8c5988ed2e38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183174}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed949a79-7417-534c-9f56-fa03f1c6bc81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183173} :answer-field/id #uuid "45888489-94ed-541f-8e81-9c58b0bb07c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183169}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a160f7a5-3a4d-5809-8834-75195b7301e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-35433" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $3\\times 6$ matrix. If $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$, find $\\text{dim(Null}(A)).$" :question/worked-solution "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=6,$ and since $B$ is formed by $3$ vectors, $\\text{rank}(A)=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 3+\\text{nullity}(A) & =6 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =6-3 \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272246 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4635be5a-a6fe-56a4-8e10-c0910f4f8147" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269912} {:answer/id #uuid "576e17c2-77cc-5c9e-8c7f-5cabff8304b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270041} {:answer/id #uuid "8f2da899-b45f-50aa-9e21-fab69ff8ebb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270515} {:answer/id #uuid "bd9f5d43-13bc-582f-a5e7-f913ae058dca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270942} {:answer/id #uuid "c477f